Definition 6 (Parallel Transport). Parallel transport is a geometric operation that moves a vector along a curve on a manifold while preserving its inner product with tangent vectors along the path. In the context of hyperbolic geometry, parallel transport ensures that vectors remain tangent to the hyperboloid model while accounting for the manifold’s curvature. It is a way to slide frames along geodesics.
